{
 "id": "2023-zhao-interior",
 "name": "Interior Materials and Home Restorativeness: Semantic Differential",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: participants see the paper's pictures of ten interior materials (wall paint, textile, wood, plastic, glass, metal, tile, brick, stone, concrete; Figure 1, CC BY 4.0, originally from Freepik) in random order and rate each material, as used in a home, on the study's 12 adjective pairs from 1 to 7, chosen for the four restorative features: being away (ordinary–special, harmony–clash), extent (textured, colorful, finished, patterned), fascination (luxury, pleasing, attractive) and compatibility (durable, solid, changeable). Scoring as in the paper: feature means; preference = fascination + compatibility; relaxation = being away + compatibility; restorative potential = all four; Wilcoxon signed-rank tests for the 45 material pairs. Original study: 85 design students and architects in China; glass ranked best, metal worst, and wood was not the most restorative.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper only says 1 is the weakest and 7 the strongest feeling; here each pair is shown as a bipolar row with the second word at 1 and the first word at 7, matching its coding (for being away a lower score means more being away); (2) materials come in random order on one screen each; (3) the intro and instruction wording are mine, following the paper's note that participants used their own experience or the Internet; (4) age, gender and a design-background question are added (the original sample was design students and architects).",
